<Learn
  title="India Atlas — Learn · Per capita: divide by the people"
  description="Learn № 15 — the economy can grow at 8 per cent while the average Indian grows slower, because population grows too. What per capita honestly tells you, and what it cannot: who actually got the growth."
  slug="per-capita"
  kicker="Learn № 15 — Reading numbers · Per capita"
  h1="Divide the growth by <i>the people.</i>"
  lede="The economy is a total; people are the denominator. Both grow. Per capita is what remains after the second growth eats part of the first, and it is the honest way to ask whether a bigger economy means better-off Indians."
  meta={[
    ["Concept", "Per capita"],
    ["Illustrated with", "A worked example"],
    ["Source", "MoSPI, NSO"],
    ["Level", "First principles"],
    ["Reading time", "5 min"],
  ]}
  dateStamp="Evergreen"
  crumbTitle="Per capita"
>

  <!-- § 01 the division -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>A total, divided</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>India's economy can grow at 8 per cent while the average Indian's share grows more slowly. Both things are true at once, and no trick is involved. The total grows. The population grows too. Per capita is what is left after the second growth eats part of the first.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Per capita <span class="sub">per head · a total divided by people</span></div>
          <p>Any total, be it GDP, income, steel or electricity, divided by the population. Per capita GDP is the economy's output shared out evenly on paper, whether or not it is shared evenly in fact.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> what each person's share would be if the total were divided equally.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">The phrase does one job superbly: it lets you compare economies of different sizes, and one economy with its own past. India's total GDP dwarfs Sri Lanka's and always will; per head, the comparison becomes a real question. Likewise across time: the economy is many times larger than it was in 1991, and the meaningful part of that claim is the growth per person.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Median <span class="sub">the middle person</span></div>
          <p>The income of the person in the middle when everyone is lined up poorest to richest: half earn more, half earn less. Unlike the average, it is unmoved by what happens at the very top.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the typical person, not the arithmetical one.</div>
        </aside>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · i</span>
          <b>Growth has two parents.</b> Total output and total population. Quote one without the other and you have told half the story.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · ii</span>
          <b>About a point a year.</b> India's population has been growing at roughly 1 per cent a year and slowing. That is the standing discount on every headline growth rate.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · iii</span>
          <b>On paper, evenly.</b> Per capita divides as if the economy were shared equally. It is a division, not a description.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 the arithmetic -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>The arithmetic, with toy numbers</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">A worked example · illustration, not data</span></div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">GDP growth · toy economy</div><div class="v">+8%</div><div class="d">the total, one year on</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Population growth · toy economy</div><div class="v">+1%</div><div class="d">the denominator grows too</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Per capita growth</div><div class="v acc">≈ +7%</div><div class="d">what each share did</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">The exact figure</div><div class="v">6.9%</div><div class="d">1.08 ÷ 1.01, minus one</div></div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:40px">Imagine a toy economy of ₹100 shared among 100 people: ₹1 per head. A year later the economy is ₹108 and the people are 101. The total grew 8 per cent, but the share per head grew to about ₹1.07, a gain of roughly 7 per cent. The exact answer is 1.08 divided by 1.01, which is 6.9 per cent. The missing point and a bit was population, doing quiet arithmetic on the headline.</p>
    <p>Over a decade the discount compounds. A country can double its economy while less than doubling the means of its people, and the gap between those two sentences is exactly population growth. This is not an argument that growth fails; it is a reminder that the headline and the household are connected by a denominator.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 what it cannot say -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>What the average cannot say</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">A limit, stated plainly</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Per capita is an honest answer to a narrow question: how much is there, per person, on paper? It cannot answer who got the growth. If one person's income doubles and everyone else's stands still, the average rises while four people out of five feel nothing. Whether growth reached the median Indian is a question about distribution, and it needs a different tool. That is the subject of the companion note on <a href="/learn/averages-and-distribution/">averages and distribution</a>.</p>
        <p>Used well, per capita is the right way to compare countries of different sizes, or a country with its own past. Used badly, it is a total wearing an individual's clothes. The economy is bigger. Whether people are better off is a separate claim, and it stands on separate evidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · iv</span>
          <b>A narrow question, honestly answered.</b> Per capita says how much exists per head. It says nothing about who holds it.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · v</span>
          <b>Two claims, not one.</b> “The economy grew” and “Indians are better off” need different evidence. Per capita speaks only to the first.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“8 per cent growth means 8 per cent for me.”</h3>
          <p>No. It means the total grew 8 per cent. Your share depends on population growth first and on distribution after that. The headline is a statement about the economy, not about you.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“A bigger economy is always a richer people.”</h3>
          <p>Not by itself. If population grows as fast as output, per capita stands still while the total doubles. Size and prosperity are different axes, and a country can move along one without moving along the other.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Per capita is the typical person's income.”</h3>
          <p>No: it is the average, and the average is nobody. The typical person is the median, who can sit far below the mean when income is concentrated at the top. The two are different instruments for different questions.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. Per capita national income is published with the national accounts, dividing GDP at constant 2011–12 prices by the official population projections. Population growth of roughly 1 per cent a year, slowing, is the standing figure behind the worked example.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>Per capita here means the mean: a total divided by a headcount. The toy figures in the worked example are chosen for legible arithmetic and are labelled as illustration, not data. What the measure cannot say, the page says it cannot say.</p>
    <p>The distribution question is real and is handled in the companion note rather than waved past here.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-note">
    <h6>This note</h6>
    <p>Learn № 15 · Reading numbers · version 1.0. Evergreen, written to survive the next print. Companion notes: <a href="/learn/averages-and-distribution/">Averages and distribution</a>, <a href="/learn/real-vs-nominal/">Real versus nominal</a>.</p>
    <p>Companion dossier: <a href="/dossiers/gdp/">Dossier 02 · National Accounts</a>.</p>
  </div>

</Learn>
